<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dance of Zalongo</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1803">1803</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Dance of Zalongo is the name given to the mass suicide of Souliote women on Mount Zalongo in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epirus">Epirus</a> in December 1803, after Ali Pasha of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ioannina">Ioannina</a> had forced Souli to surrender. According to tradition, the women, to avoid capture by his troops, threw their children and then themselves from the cliffs while dancing and singing. The event became a symbol of Greek resistance and is marked by a monument on the site.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dance-of-zalongo.jpg" alt="Dance of Zalongo" width="320" height="232" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ary Scheffer (Life time: 1795-1858) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Women_of_Souli_(Scheffer).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>In December 1803, during the Souliote War, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/souliotes">Souliotes</a> began to leave Souli after their defeat by the forces of Ali Pasha, the local Ottoman-Albanian ruler. Some of the refugees made for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parga">Parga</a>, while others sought shelter at the convent of Zalongo. Approximately 60 women, with their children, were trapped on Mount Zalongo. The war gave rise to many legends, and the events are told differently depending on the version.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Versions that rely on the History of Suli and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parga">Parga</a> by Christoforos Perraivos present Ali as a cruel tyrant who broke a promise of protection to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/souliotes">Souliotes</a> who had withdrawn to Zalongo and ordered his men to attack; the men prepared to fight to the death, while the women, to avoid enslavement, formed a circle dance and went over the cliff. The Alipashiad, which does not hide that many Souliotes were enslaved, stresses instead Ali's honourable dealings with the Souliote chief Fotos Tzavelas, whose wife he released and whom he allowed safe passage to Parga with a number of families.</p><p>William Plate of the Royal Geographical Society suggested that Ali was angered by the monk known as Last Judgement Samuel, who with five others blew up the Souliote arsenal with Ali's soldiers inside, and that he took revenge on the fugitives. In this account the convent of Zalongo was stormed and its defenders killed, and those who escaped climbed to the top of the mountain, where, according to legend, they went down the precipice one after the other, singing and dancing.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The story became known across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and reached the English-speaking world in the 1820s, when it was used in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> to win sympathy for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greek-war-of-independence">Greek War of Independence</a>. Ary Scheffer showed The Women of Souli at the Paris Salon of 1827, and Constance Blanchard exhibited her painting of the scene there in 1838. During that war, after the siege of Naoussa, thirteen women and their children leapt to their deaths into the Arapitsa in the same way. A popular kalamatianos dance-song comes from a drama by Spyridon Peresiadis, published in 1903, and the Monument of Zalongo now stands on the mountain.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Dance of Zalongo” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Many Souliotes went to Corfu, while women reportedly threw themselves from the cliffs at <b>Zalongo</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/souliote-confederacy">Souliote Confederacy</a></li></ul></section>
